    use:"Why lithium metal is the prize and the problem, and how much excess lithium a real cell can afford.",
    url:"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41560-017-0047-2" },
  { n:10, tag:"Nat. Mater. 2011", txt:"Kamaya, N.; Homma, K.; Yamakawa, Y.; Hirayama, M.; Kanno, R.; Yonemura, M.; Kamiyama, T.; Kato, Y.; Hama, S.; Kawamoto, K.; Mitsui, A. A lithium superionic conductor. <em>Nat. Mater.</em> <b>2011</b>, <em>10</em> (9), 682–686.",
    use:"Sulfide conductivity: Li₁₀GeP₂S₁₂ at 1.2 × 10⁻² S/cm — the reason sulfide leads on ionic conductivity.",
    url:"https://doi.org/10.1038/nmat3066" },
  { n:11, tag:"Angew. Chem. 2007", txt:"Murugan, R.; Thangadurai, V.; Weppner, W. Fast Lithium Ion Conduction in Garnet-Type Li₇La₃Zr₂O₁₂. <em>Angew. Chem. Int. Ed.</em> <b>2007</b>, <em>46</em> (41), 7778–7781.",
    use:"Oxide conductivity: garnet LLZO at about 3 × 10⁻⁴ S/cm, roughly an order below sulfide.",
    url:"https://doi.org/10.1002/anie.200701144" },
  { n:12, tag:"Commun. Chem. 2023", txt:"Klimpel, M.; Zhang, H.; Kovalenko, M. V.; Kravchyk, K. V. Standardizing critical current density measurements in lithium garnets. <em>Commun. Chem.</em> <b>2023</b>, <em>6</em>, 192.",
    use:"Critical current density in garnet is commonly quoted near 0.3–1 mA/cm², but there is no standard protocol — reported values vary widely.",
